 ## Overview
-Deep Reflectance Volumes (DRV) is a differentiable volume rendering method that reconstructs scene appearance from unstructured images captured under collocated point lighting [S1]. It represents a scene using three voxel grids—opacity, surface normal, and reflectance parameters—and optimizes these volumes by minimizing the error between rendered and captured images [S1]. Unlike previous view synthesis methods that accumulate opacity only along view directions, DRV learns global scene opacity accumulated from both view and light directions to compute accurate hard shadows, and unlike volume-based works that learn a single color per voxel, it reconstructs pervoxel reflectance to handle complex materials with high glossiness [S1].
+Deep Reflectance Volumes (DRV) is a differentiable volume rendering method that reconstructs scene appearance from unstructured images captured under collocated point lighting by optimizing a volumetric scene representation [S1]. It represents a scene using three distinct voxel grids: opacity ($\alpha$), surface normal ($\mathbf{n}$), and reflectance parameters ($R$) [S1]. Unlike standard volume rendering which often assumes a single color per voxel or accumulates opacity only along view directions, DRV accumulates global scene opacity from both view and light directions to compute accurate hard shadows and models single-bounce light transport with complex, spatially-varying reflectance (e.g., high glossiness) [S1]. It builds on [Inverse Rendering and Optimization](8999-inverse-rendering-and-optimization.md) by providing a physically-based differentiable ray marching framework that supports arbitrary viewpoint and lighting at inference time, despite being trained only on collocated setups [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of unstructured images captured under collocated point lighting.
-*   **Outputs:** Optimized voxel grids for opacity ($\alpha$), normal ($\mathbf{n}$), and reflectance parameters ($R$) in a transformed space, which are warped to world space for rendering.
-
-**Data Structures:**
